          <Markdown id="transcript_md1">
## Review community focus group data
### Focus Group Transcript
**Interviewer:** 
Thank you for participating in this focus group and thank you for providing your consent to have this meeting recorded. Let's begin. Our community needs survey identified high school dropout rates as an issue of concern. The purpose of this focus group is to discuss causes of dropping out of high school. Why do you think students in our community dropout of high school? 

**Participant 1:**
I'll start. My kids all graduated from high school. Whenever they complained or they felt like quitting, we kept on them to keep at it. Luckily, so did their friends. Everybody around them just wouldn't let them quit. While they did not get along with all their teachers, they had some FANTASTIC teachers who supported them. So, we all helped them to stick it out. 

**Participant 2:**
Those are some good friends to have! I'm not always sure what influence my daughter's friends are having on her - good or bad. 

**Interviewer 1:**
Thank you for sharing your experiences. But for now, I'd like us to focus on the causes leading to students dropping out. Later, we can discuss the kinds of actions and support that can help them to persist to graduation. 

**Participant 3:**
I have an experience to share. My daughter's best friend has dyslexia and was falling behind in all her classes. Her grades were awful. And she wasn't getting the help that she needed from the school to catch up. When my daughter was a sophomore, her friend dropped out because she couldn't cope. 

**Participant 1:**
That probably wasn't easy for your daughter or her friend. 

**Participant 3:**
Yes, my daughter was really upset. She knew her friend since elementary school. Thankfully, her friend was able to get tutoring at a community center that helped her improve her reading and she eventually got her GED. 

**Participant 2:**
If our schools had more tutoring or mentorship programs, we might be able to help our kids before they drop out, instead of after it is too late. 

**Interviewer:**
Thanks for that suggestion, but if you don't mind holding off on those kinds of ideas until we get to that part of the focus group, I'll bring that up again for more discussion. 

**Participant 2:**
Oh, sure. No problem. 

**Participant 4:**
Here's something - kind of big picture, but I know   some parents who still believe that graduating from high school isn't as important or as high a priority as supporting the family. 

**Interviewer:**
Can you tell us more about that? 

**Participant 4:**
Well, I know this because of some of my neighbors. One of my son's friend's parents had steady, good paying jobs for years working in their family's restaurant. Another family has a small business and expect their kids to take it over. These parents did not have or need high school degrees to get those jobs. They want their children to follow in their footsteps. 

So, whether it is to work in the family business or just to take care of an elder family member instead of going to school, they tell me that's more important for their children to help out than getting a high school diploma. 

**Interviewer:**
Thank you. The values and beliefs of families can be complicated influences on their children. 

**Participant 5:**
I don't know if this is appropriate, but I do know that   behavioral and especially legal issues can lead to dropping out. 

**Interviewer:**
Can you tell us more about that? 

**Participant 5:**
I work in a community legal office. And, well, sometimes I am called in to represent young people who get in trouble with the law or arrested. It could be getting caught shoplifting or something more serious like ...well, you can imagine. Whether they are eventually found guilty or innocent, it can have an impact on completing school to graduation. The entire experience with the legal system can take time and resources, creating stress on the youth and families. Then, the youth faces the social pressures in school from friends, classmates, and teachers, even when they are trying to be supportive. 

And in some schools with no-tolerance policies, once school administrators find out about the allegations, the student may be expelled. 

A young adult may feel trapped and dropping out may seem like the best option. 

**Participant 3:**
Mmmmm, yeah, I can see that. 

**Interviewer:**
Are there any other comments on the causes? [interviewer pause] OK, let's move on to the next part of the focus group.
       </Markdown>
    </Annotate>
    <Ref target="focus_group" />
    </Vertical>

